[9b1aee44f3bec2ac] (mini.criticism.v1)
All three proposals in this cycle executed as expected: each correctly predicted that conformance.kernel.recovery would move when the fence structure changed. The proposers accurately read the documented rule: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text' [4adba2f4da302fee]. The source code implements this by iterating 'for pool in (fenced, top_level)' and returning the first dict found in reversed order [1a49f5929bf26106]. No failures occurred in this cycle—the rule as written and the code part company nowhere in these executions. A proposer should continue testing edge cases where fences contain mixed prose and objects, or where multiple fences compete, since the evidence catalogue shows recovery behaviour varies across transforms [638a6360b40e8b8a][e847048a8c7847a8].

